---
description: >
  Implementation plan for CLI TUI enhancements — description-first tree summaries, type-branched detail panel for plan/todo files, and aide_discover parity (status flags, descriptions, wider tree panel).
intent: >
  Enhance the TUI so the tree panel shows frontmatter descriptions as node
  summaries (unconditionally, not gated behind deep view), the detail panel
  renders plan and todo files with their own layouts instead of the generic
  intent-card, and the tree renders the same information agents receive from
  aide_discover — descriptions, alignment status flags, and file types.
---

## Plan

### 1. [x] Add `description` field to AideFile and populate it during scan

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions.md`, `coding-playbook/architecture/folder-structure.md`

- Modify `src/types/index.ts`: add an optional `description?: string` field to the `AideFile` interface. This field holds the frontmatter `description` when present.
- Modify `src/util/scan/index.ts`: after the existing `extractSummary` call (which reads body text), also parse frontmatter via `parseFrontmatter` from `@/util/parseFrontmatter/index.js` to extract `description` and `status`. Store both on the `AideFile` being pushed. The `description` field gets the frontmatter `description` value (or empty string if absent). This means every `AideFile` now carries both the body-derived `summary` and the frontmatter-derived `description`.
- Also add an optional `status?: "aligned" | "misaligned"` field to `AideFile` in `src/types/index.ts` and populate it from frontmatter during scan. This supports the status flag rendering in step 4.
- The `parseFrontmatter` import is already available in the codebase. In scan's `walk` function, read the file content (which already happens for the non-shallow case) and parse frontmatter from it. For the shallow case, the file is not read at all today — but descriptions should appear unconditionally (not gated behind deep view). Therefore, in the shallow case, read only the first ~500 bytes of each file (enough to capture frontmatter) and parse `description` and `status` from it. The `summary` field stays empty in shallow mode (existing behavior).
- Write `src/util/scan/index.test.ts`: test that scan populates `description` from frontmatter when present, leaves it empty when frontmatter has no `description`, and populates `status` when present. Use a temp directory with fixture `.aide` files.

### 2. [x] Tree panel: show descriptions unconditionally and widen to 55-60%

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions.md`, `coding-playbook/architecture/folder-structure.md`

- 2a. Modify `src/cli/TreePanel/index.tsx` — `renderRow` function (lines 64-91): Change the summary display logic. Currently summary shows only when `isDeepView && file.summary`. New behavior: if `file.description` is non-empty, show it unconditionally (regardless of `isDeepView`). If `file.description` is empty but `isDeepView` is true and `file.summary` exists, fall back to `file.summary`. The description text uses the same ` — {text}` format and gray color as existing summaries. Also, if `file.status` is set, render a status badge after the type tag: `[aligned]` in green or `[misaligned]` in red.
- 2b. Modify `src/cli/App/index.tsx` — the layout section (line 312): Change the tree width calculation from `Math.floor(columns * 0.38)` to `Math.floor(columns * 0.57)`. This gives the tree panel ~57% of terminal width so descriptions are readable alongside node paths and type tags. The detail panel gets the remaining ~43% via `flexGrow={1}`.
- 2c. Verify the `TreePanelProps` interface does not need changes — it already receives `isDeepView` and `width`. The `FlatNode` type wraps `TreeNode` which wraps `AideFile`, so the new `description` and `status` fields flow through automatically.

### 3. [x] Detail panel: add plan layout renderer

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions.md`, `coding-playbook/workflow/testing.md`

Create a new helper component at `src/cli/DetailPanel/renderPlanDetail/index.tsx` that renders plan files in both preview and drill-in modes.

- **Preview mode**: Show the plan's `description` (from frontmatter), a progress bar or fraction showing checklist completion (e.g., "3/5 complete"), and the count of remaining items.
- **Drill-in mode**: Show the full list of checklist items parsed from the plan body. Each item shows its checkbox state (`[x]` rendered as a green checkmark, `[ ]` as a gray empty box). Group items by their `### N.` step headings. Show a completion summary at the top (e.g., "Progress: 3/5 steps complete").
- The plan body format is markdown with `### N. Step title` headings and `- [x]`/`- [ ]` checklist items. Parse these from the `sections` array (which `parseBody` already splits on `##` headings) or from the raw body content passed through props.
- Create a `parsePlanItems` helper at `src/cli/DetailPanel/renderPlanDetail/parsePlanItems/index.ts` that takes the body string (or sections) and returns a structured array of `{ step: string; items: { text: string; done: boolean }[] }`. This is pure parsing logic, no React.
- Write `src/cli/DetailPanel/renderPlanDetail/parsePlanItems/index.test.ts`: test parsing of plan body with mixed checked/unchecked items, step headings, and edge cases (no items, all complete, all incomplete).

### 4. [x] Detail panel: add todo layout renderer

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions.md`, `coding-playbook/workflow/testing.md`

Create a new helper component at `src/cli/DetailPanel/renderTodoDetail/index.tsx` that renders todo files in both preview and drill-in modes.

- **Preview mode**: Show the todo's `description` (from frontmatter), the count of issues, and a count of misalignment annotations if any.
- **Drill-in mode**: Show the full issue list. Each issue is a checklist item from the todo body. Items with misalignment annotations (text containing `[misaligned]` or similar markers) are highlighted in yellow/red. Items marked complete are shown with green checkmarks.
- Create a `parseTodoItems` helper at `src/cli/DetailPanel/renderTodoDetail/parseTodoItems/index.ts` that takes the body string and returns a structured array of `{ text: string; done: boolean; misaligned: boolean }`.
- Write `src/cli/DetailPanel/renderTodoDetail/parseTodoItems/index.test.ts`: test parsing of todo body with misalignment annotations, mixed states, and edge cases.

### 5. [x] Wire type-branched rendering into DetailPanel

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions.md`, `coding-playbook/architecture/folder-structure.md`

- Modify `src/cli/DetailPanel/index.tsx`: Add a file-type branching check at the top of both preview and drill-in rendering paths. When `file.type` is `"plan"`, delegate to the `renderPlanDetail` component (from step 3). When `file.type` is `"todo"`, delegate to the `renderTodoDetail` component (from step 4). When `file.type` is `"intent"` or `"research"`, use the existing intent-card layout (scope, intent, outcomes).
- Import the new renderer components from their subfolder paths.
- Pass through the necessary props: `file`, `frontmatter`, `mode`, `sections`, `expandedSection`, `drilledFilePath`.
- The existing `OutcomeList` and `OutcomesDisplay` helpers stay in DetailPanel — they are only used by the intent/research rendering path and are small enough to remain in the same file per the single-helper exception.

### 6. [x] Tree panel: render status flags on dir nodes

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions.md`

- Modify `src/cli/TreePanel/index.tsx` — the dir-node branch of `renderRow` (lines 50-62): When a dir node's primary intent file has a status, show a status badge next to the dir label. This requires access to the file data within dir nodes. The `TreeNode` type for dir nodes already has `children: TreeNode[]` — find the first `"intent"` child and check its `status` field. Render `[aligned]` in green or `[misaligned]` in red after the dir path.
- Also show the dir node's primary intent `description` when available (similar logic: find the first intent child, use its `description`). This gives dir nodes the same information density as aide_discover's ancestor chain, where each level shows its description.

### 7. [x] Update search filter to include descriptions

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions.md`

- Modify `src/cli/App/index.tsx` — the `hasMatchingDescendant` function and the `visibleNodes` filter: Include `file.description` in the search match logic alongside `file.relativePath` and `file.summary`. This ensures that typing a search term that appears in a description still surfaces the correct nodes.

## Decisions

### Description takes precedence over body-derived summary
The spec explicitly states that frontmatter `description` should take precedence over body-derived summaries when present. Rather than replacing `summary` with `description` on the `AideFile` type, both fields are kept. `description` comes from frontmatter (always populated even in shallow scan), `summary` comes from body text (only in deep scan). The tree panel uses `description` first, falling back to `summary` in deep view. This preserves backward compatibility and avoids breaking the MCP discover tool's existing behavior.

### Descriptions shown unconditionally (not gated behind deep view)
The spec is explicit: "Descriptions appear unconditionally in the tree, not gated behind the deep-view toggle." This means the shallow scan must read enough of each file to extract frontmatter — but NOT the body. The implementation reads only the first ~500 bytes (enough for the YAML block) in shallow mode, keeping startup fast. `summary` remains empty in shallow mode (existing behavior). Per playbook convention `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions.md`: investigate first, never apply quick hacks — the shallow scan reads frontmatter rather than deferring description to deep mode.

### Tree width increased to 57% (from 38%)
The spec calls for 55-60% tree width to make descriptions readable. 57% (0.57 multiplier) is chosen as the midpoint. The detail panel remains `flexGrow={1}` to use the remaining space. This matches the spec's strategy section: "The tree panel gets roughly 55-60% of terminal width."

### Plan and todo get their own renderers as subfolder helpers
Per progressive disclosure (`progressive-disclosure.md`) and the orchestrator/helper pattern (`coding-playbook/architecture/folder-structure.md`), the plan and todo renderers are extracted into their own subfolders under `DetailPanel/` rather than inlined as conditionals in the main component. Each renderer owns its own parsing logic (also in a subfolder). This keeps `DetailPanel/index.tsx` as the orchestrator that delegates based on file type.

### Status field added to AideFile
The spec requires parity with aide_discover output, which shows alignment status flags from frontmatter. Adding `status` to `AideFile` (populated during scan) is the cleanest path — it flows through `TreeNode` and `FlatNode` without changes to those types. The alternative (reading frontmatter on-demand in the tree panel) would require async rendering in the tree component, which Ink does not support well.

### Parser helpers are pure functions with their own tests
`parsePlanItems` and `parseTodoItems` are pure data transformers (string in, structured array out) — no React, no I/O. They live in their own subfolders per the orchestrator/helper pattern and get their own co-located tests per `coding-playbook/workflow/testing.md`. This makes them independently verifiable without rendering Ink components.
